{
  "dom_id": "step-q98552",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-1.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,-3).$",
  "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,-3)+∇f(-2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x+2,y+3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-2,-3)=-19.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\, &  & ⇒\\,{f}_{x}(-2,-3)=(-3)^{2}=9, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =2xy &  & ⇒\\,{f}_{y}(-2,-3)=2(-2)(-3)=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-2,-3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,-3) & =⟨9,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,-3)+∇\\,f(-2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+3⟩ \\\\  & =-19+⟨9,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+3⟩ \\\\  & =-19+9(x+2)+12(y+3) \\\\  & =35+9x+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
